# tgloggerd webapi

The Go web service of tgloggerd. The web backend moves from the Drogon
application in web/ to this service, one feature at a time
(docs/design/web-go.md). While both run, nginx sends /api/ here and
everything else to Drogon. Both read the same databases with the same
users and grants.

So far the service serves one endpoint:

    GET /api/health   200 {"status":"ok","checks":{"ro":"ok","app":"ok"}}
                      503 when a pool does not answer; that pool's check
                      reads "unavailable" and the reason is logged.

It needs no login and exists for probes and for checking the routing.

# Layout

    go.mod                 Module tgloggerd/webapi and its requirements.
    go.sum                 Checksums of the required modules.
    cmd/webapi/            main: configuration, the two pools, the HTTP
                           server, shutdown on SIGTERM.
    internal/config/       Settings from the environment.
    internal/server/       Routes and handlers.
    vendor/                The third-party modules, committed.
    build/                 Build output (git-ignored).

Routes use the standard library's ServeMux with method and path
patterns ("GET /api/health"). Add a third-party router only with a
reason it cannot give.

# The module path names no host

The module path is `tgloggerd/webapi`, and the service's own imports are
`tgloggerd/webapi/...`. It must never start with a repository host
and account, the way third-party paths do: the code is the same wherever a copy of the
repository is hosted, and moving it changes nothing. Third-party modules
keep their upstream paths; those are the only host names under webapi/,
in go.mod, go.sum and vendor/. To check:

    grep -rnE '[a-z0-9-]+\.(com|org|net|io|dev)/' webapi \
        --exclude-dir=vendor --exclude=go.sum

must print only the third-party require lines of go.mod and the
imports of those modules.

# Build and run

In the compose deployment the "webapi" service (container
tgloggerd-webapi) runs `run.sh webapi`, which builds this directory into
webapi/build/webapi with the toolchain pinned in the Dockerfile and
execs it. The build uses only vendor/ (GOFLAGS=-mod=vendor, GOPROXY=off),
so it never reaches the network. The container is not published to the
host; nginx reaches it on its tgnet address (see web/README.web).

By hand, from this directory, with the same Go version:

    GOFLAGS=-mod=vendor GOPROXY=off go build -o build/webapi ./cmd/webapi
    go vet ./...

The service applies no migrations: the web service owns the web schema
and the daemon the logger's.

# Configuration

The database settings are the Drogon web's, with the same defaults, so
one .env serves both (see .env.example):

    WEB_DB_HOST, WEB_DB_PORT     the MySQL server; fall back to
                                 TG_DB_HOST, TG_DB_PORT
    WEB_DB_RO_NAME, _USER, _PASSWORD, _CONNS
                                 the read-only pool over the logger's
                                 schema
    WEB_DB_APP_NAME, _USER, _PASSWORD, _CONNS
                                 the read-write pool over the web's own
                                 schema

and its own:

    WEBAPI_LISTEN_ADDR   default 127.0.0.1
    WEBAPI_LISTEN_PORT   default 8081
    WEBAPI_LOG_LEVEL     debug, info (default), warn or error

An empty value counts as unset. A malformed number stops the service at
start. Logs are structured (log/slog, key=value) on stderr, one line per
request.

On SIGTERM (docker stop) or SIGINT the service stops accepting
connections, lets requests in flight finish for up to 8 seconds, closes
the pools and exits 0.

# Updating the vendored modules

Third-party modules are few and each needs a reason. Adding or updating
one is the only step that uses the network, and the result is committed:

    go get <module>@<version>    # or edit go.mod
    go mod tidy
    go mod vendor
    go build ./... && go vet ./...

Commit go.mod, go.sum and vendor/ together, and add any new module and
its license to the third-party list in LICENSE. Run it with the same Go
version as the image, with GOFLAGS unset (-mod=vendor would stop go get).
The go directive in go.mod is the oldest Go the code and its modules
need, not the image's version; raising the image's toolchain does not
change it.
